import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"ADR-0154: Typecheck semantics — identity, equality, universes, literals, erasure","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0154-typecheck-semantics.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0154-typecheck-semantics.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0154-typecheck-semantics.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0154-typecheck-semantics-—-identity-equality-universes-literals-erasure" tabindex="-1">ADR-0154: Typecheck semantics — identity, equality, universes, literals, erasure <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0154-typecheck-semantics-—-identity-equality-universes-literals-erasure" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0154: Typecheck semantics — identity, equality, universes, literals, erasure”">​</a></h1><ul><li>Status: Accepted</li><li>Date: 2026-10-22</li><li>Repos: <code>kernel</code> (<code>handl-value</code>, <code>handl-language</code>), <code>kit-prelude-lang</code>, <code>engine-typecheck-nbe</code>, <code>lib-type</code></li></ul><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0106 made <code>sym()</code>/<code>sym(A)</code> the nominal-identity primitive and declared the sym parent graph the subtyping relation; ADR-0152/0153 made <code>data</code>, <code>~[n]~&gt;</code>, and <code>f&lt;…&gt;</code> one indexed-former mechanism. Review of those decisions surfaced five semantics questions that ADR-0106 left open or conflated. This ADR settles them. None adds machinery — each pins down how the existing primitives are <em>read</em>.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-deterministic-sym-seeding-for-declared-types" tabindex="-1">1. Deterministic <code>sym</code> seeding for declared types <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-deterministic-sym-seeding-for-declared-types" aria-label="Permalink to “1. Deterministic sym seeding for declared types”">​</a></h3><p><code>sym()</code> mints fresh identity per evaluation — correct for locals, but a declared former minted at module-eval time breaks equality across import paths and processes: <code>Vec</code> loaded twice is two identities.</p><p>Exported/declared formers mint <strong>deterministically</strong>: <code>sym</code> seeded by the module-qualified name (<code>sym(&quot;vec.Vec&quot;)</code> — or the content hash once ADR-0010 addressing lands). The seed is the identity; minting the same name is idempotent, not fresh. Plain <code>sym()</code> stays non-deterministic for locals. This is what lets nominal types cross the artifact cache (ADR-0065), separate compilation, and FFI boundaries.</p><h3 id="_2-membership-and-subtyping-are-two-links-not-one-chain" tabindex="-1">2. Membership and subtyping are two links, not one chain <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-membership-and-subtyping-are-two-links-not-one-chain" aria-label="Permalink to “2. Membership and subtyping are two links, not one chain”">​</a></h3><p>A single parent chain conflates <code>x : T</code> (membership) with <code>T &lt;: U</code> (subtyping) — transitively it makes <code>Zero instanceof Type</code> true, since <code>Type</code> tops every type&#39;s chain. The split:</p><ul><li>values carry a <code>type</code> link → their type&#39;s sym;</li><li>types carry a <code>parent</code> link → their supertype sym;</li><li><code>x : T</code> reads <code>x.type</code>; <code>T &lt;: U</code> walks <code>parent</code>; <code>instanceof</code> = <code>type</code> link then parent-walk.</li></ul><p><code>x : T</code> propagates through supertypes (<code>x : Nat</code>, <code>Nat &lt;: Int</code> ⇒ <code>x : Int</code>) as a <em>judgment</em> the checker derives, not a single walk.</p><h3 id="_3-equality-law-—-structural-by-default-sym-opts-out" tabindex="-1">3. Equality law — structural by default, <code>sym</code> opts out <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-equality-law-—-structural-by-default-sym-opts-out" aria-label="Permalink to “3. Equality law — structural by default, sym opts out”">​</a></h3><p><code>==</code> is structural equality over immutable data: two objects with the same content are the same value, everywhere, in every module. <code>sym</code> is the <em>only</em> escape — identical shape, distinct identity.</p><p>Consequently: record/tuple/<code>Pi</code>/<code>Sigma</code> types and former applications (<code>Vec(2,Int)</code> = <code>{former, args}</code>) compare structurally; declared <code>data</code> formers compare nominally. <code>Nat ≠ OtherNat</code> even when both are <code>Zero | Succ</code> — nominal heads, structural bodies.</p><h3 id="_4-universes-are-indexed-type-i-type-i-1" tabindex="-1">4. Universes are indexed: <code>Type(i) : Type(i+1)</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-universes-are-indexed-type-i-type-i-1" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Universes are indexed: Type(i) : Type(i+1)”">​</a></h3><p><code>Type : Type</code> admits Girard&#39;s paradox — with proofs as real values, every <code>Path(a,b)</code> becomes inhabitable and equations lose meaning. The paradox is a <em>checker</em> property (the kernel sees only syms), so the fix is a checker rule:</p><ul><li><code>Type</code> is an indexed former: <code>Type(i) : Type(i+1)</code>; bare <code>Type</code> = <code>Type(0)</code>. <code>Obj</code> is the kernel world, not a type — <code>Type(i) : Obj</code>, and <code>Obj</code> has no level to check.</li><li><code>Pi</code>&#39;s level is <code>max(domain, codomain)</code> — the existing <code>universe_max</code> judgment op. <code>Pi(A : Type, A ~&gt; A)</code> is <code>Type(1)</code> by construction; self-quantification is structurally unwritable.</li><li>Levels infer like other indices — invisible in ordinary code; only type-quantifying declarations surface them.</li></ul><h3 id="_5-numeric-literals-are-constrained-metas" tabindex="-1">5. Numeric literals are constrained metas <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-numeric-literals-are-constrained-metas" aria-label="Permalink to “5. Numeric literals are constrained metas”">​</a></h3><p>A literal (<code>1</code>, <code>3.14</code>) synthesizes a metavariable constrained to a descendant of <code>type.number</code>/<code>type.float</code> — it unifies with the first concrete numeric type it meets (<code>1 : Float</code> solves <code>?l := Float</code>, not via <code>Nat &lt;: Float</code>, which is false — Nat&#39;s parent is Int). Unresolved metas default: integer literals → <code>Nat</code>, fraction literals → <code>Float</code>. <code>-1</code> is unary negation applied to a <code>Nat</code> — the <em>application</em> is <code>Int</code>-typed. No literal-specific typing rules; reuse the meta store.</p><h3 id="_6-erasure-is-a-pipeline-property-verified-by-an-artifact-lint" tabindex="-1">6. Erasure is a pipeline property, verified by an artifact lint <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-erasure-is-a-pipeline-property-verified-by-an-artifact-lint" aria-label="Permalink to “6. Erasure is a pipeline property, verified by an artifact lint”">​</a></h3><p>No quantity system on binders (QTT-style <code>0/1/ω</code> is rejected — the <code>~[n]~&gt;</code> bracket is dimension, per ADR-0153). Instead:</p><ul><li>Elaboration consumes all type-level content — metas solved, indices normalized, <code>match</code> lowered to <code>F.rec</code>, annotations erased per ADR-0151. <code>exec-transform</code> dead-code passes then drop anything only higher cells referenced.</li><li><strong>Invariant</strong>: the final runnable artifact contains only 1-arrows + runtime values — no former applications, no metas, no undecided indices. A lint pass asserts this post-pipeline: stuck-but-referenced terms are <em>live</em> code DCE cannot remove — the lint is what turns &quot;stuck&quot; from silent unsafety into a diagnostic (pipeline bug or a needed runtime backstop).</li><li>Runtime-minted cells (<code>eval</code>/<code>resolve</code> constructing a law or type at runtime) are <strong>allowed</strong> — laws-as-values is a feature; erasure is best-effort for them, not guaranteed.</li></ul><h3 id="_7-types-yaml-is-a-dispatch-table-not-the-spec" tabindex="-1">7. <code>types.yaml</code> is a dispatch table, not the spec <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-types-yaml-is-a-dispatch-table-not-the-spec" aria-label="Permalink to “7. types.yaml is a dispatch table, not the spec”">​</a></h3><p>The honest split: <code>lang.types[kind]</code> maps node kinds to named judgment ops (<code>synth_*</code>/<code>check_*</code>); the ops — implemented in Rust, becoming <code>.hnl</code> when the ADR-0105 elaborator returns — are the semantic core and the normative spec. Simple kinds keep combinator schemes; the file never pretends to be the checker.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Seeding and links are phase-1 work</strong> — they shape <code>handl-value</code>&#39;s <code>Val</code> domain; cheap now, structural rewrites later.</li><li><strong><code>Type(i)</code> inference</strong> reuses the existing <code>universe_level</code>/<code>max</code> ops; no new judgment machinery.</li><li><strong>Literal metas</strong> need the constraint field on metas (&quot;must solve to a descendant of X&quot;) — one small addition to the meta store.</li><li><strong>The lint</strong> rides <code>exec-transform</code> passes already in place; it is a verification pass, not a typing discipline.</li><li>Together with 0152/0153 this closes the semantics: syms + indexed formers + one equality law + stratified <code>Type</code> + pipeline erasure.</li></ul><h2 id="references" tabindex="-1">References <a class="header-anchor" href="#references" aria-label="Permalink to “References”">​</a></h2><ul><li>ADR-0106 (sym kernel), §14 (subtyping), §18 (runtime/static convergence) — refined by §§1–2 here</li><li>ADR-0152 (<code>data</code> formers), ADR-0153 (dimension index)</li><li>ADR-0151 (annotation erasure at exec), ADR-0032 (proof erasure)</li><li>Girard&#39;s paradox (why <code>Type : Type</code> is excluded); Kovács smalltt (NbE conventions the <code>Val</code> domain follows)</li></ul>`,29)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};